Optional upstream context test configuration (optctx/)
======================================================

The 'optctx' test traces a request whether or not it arrives with a W3C trace
context.  A request that carries a valid 'traceparent' continues the trace it
names, a request that carries none, or one the propagator cannot read, opens a
trace of its own.  This is the shape an HAProxy at the edge needs, where part of
the traffic comes from instrumented senders and the rest from browsers, health
checkers and hand-written clients.

A 'span' line takes no 'if'/'unless' condition, so the two root spans stand in
two scopes bound to one event, and an ACL over the 'otel.context' fetch decides
which of them runs.  The fetch answers only once the extraction has run, which
is why the 'extract' keeps a scope of its own, placed first; scopes run in the
order their sections appear in the file.

This is the opposite of 'option require-context', which suppresses the telemetry
of a stream that brings no context instead of tracing it on its own.


Files
-----

  optctx/otel.cfg    OTel filter configuration (two conditional root spans)
  optctx/haproxy.cfg HAProxy frontend/backend configuration
  optctx/otel.yml    Exporter, processor, reader and provider definitions


Events
------

  on-frontend-http-request  read by four scopes: the extraction, the two root
                            span branches and the request span they share
  on-http-response          adds the response span
  on-server-session-end     closes the spans and the extracted context

  The closing waits for the on-server-session-end event, because a 'finish *' on
  the response event would drop the context before the HTTP response rules of
  the frontend, the ones that read it through 'otel.context', have run.


The decision
------------

  The context name is written with a leading '-', which keeps the name out of
  the headers and reads the bare 'traceparent' and 'tracestate' that an outside
  sender emits:

    acl otel-ctx-valid otel.context(-otel-ctx)

    otel-scope http_request_extract
        extract "-otel-ctx" use-headers
        otel-event on-frontend-http-request

    otel-scope http_request_child
        span "HAProxy session" parent "-otel-ctx" root
        otel-event on-frontend-http-request if otel-ctx-valid

    otel-scope http_request_new
        span "HAProxy session" root
        otel-event on-frontend-http-request unless otel-ctx-valid

  The fetch reports the validity of the extracted context, not its presence,
  so a malformed 'traceparent' takes the second branch as well.  A '-m found'
  match would not separate those two cases, since with a name that carries no
  prefix the extraction registers a context whatever the request holds, and the
  match then reads as true even for a request without any trace headers.

  Each branch marks its span with a 'haproxy.trace_continued' attribute, and
  the frontend repeats the decision in an 'X-Otel-Trace-Continued' response
  header, so a plain curl shows which one ran.


Sampling
--------

  The SDK sampler is 'parent_based' with an 'always_on' delegate, which is what
  this configuration needs, since a continued trace then follows the decision
  its sender made and a trace opened here is sampled.

  Keep the 'rate-limit' at 100.0 and sample in the SDK instead when the sender
  is another HAProxy running this filter.  A rate limit below 100.0 skips the
  filter for part of the streams, so the sender injects no context for them
  and this configuration answers every one of those with a trace of its own.
  A sampler drops the span instead of the filter, the context still travels
  with the sampled flag cleared, and the two hosts stay on one trace.


Running the test
----------------

The run-optctx.sh script (a symlink to run-test-config.sh) runs this
configuration.  It invokes haproxy with -f haproxy-common.cfg -f
optctx/haproxy.cfg, the same as the manual command shown below.  From the
test/ directory:

  % ./run-optctx.sh

Or run it manually:

  % /path/to/haproxy -f haproxy-common.cfg -f optctx/haproxy.cfg

Then drive one request of each kind and read the response header that reports
the branch:

  % curl -sSD- -o /dev/null -H \
      'traceparent: 00-11111111111111111111111111111111-2222222222222222-01' \
      http://127.0.0.1:10080/
  % curl -sSD- -o /dev/null http://127.0.0.1:10080/

The first request produces a "HAProxy session" span in trace 1111...1111 with
2222222222222222 as its parent, the second one a span in a trace of its own
with an empty parent.
